N('stegner-house-eastend','Wallace Stegner House','Eastend','sk','landmark','standing',49.5169,-108.826,'exact','view','drive','','1916–',
  'A small wooden house built in 1916 at 126 Tamarack Avenue, where the writer Wallace Stegner lived with his family until 1921. He wrote about Eastend as "Whitemud" in Wolf Willow and other books. The town made it a municipal heritage property in 1988, and since 1989 the Eastend Arts Council has run it as a house where writers and artists stay for a month.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey frame house with a large upstairs window that faces west toward the east end of the Cypress Hills, which the register names as part of what shaped Stegner\'s writing. It stands on its original lot. Interior not open to visitors that a source says: the house is let to the resident writer or artist.',
  'The house a Pulitzer-winning writer remembered as the prairie town of Wolf Willow.',
  'The house is run by the Eastend Arts Council as a residence for visiting writers and artists (stegnerhouse.ca), so look at it from Tamarack Avenue and do not go to the door. Parking on the street (not checked on site). No hours: it is not a museum.',
  [HP('Stegner Residence',1543),L('Wallace Stegner House (the Arts Council\'s page)','https://stegnerhouse.ca/about'),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Wallace Stegner House','https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/listings/3661/wallace-stegner-house')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('fort-san-sanatorium','Fort San tuberculosis sanatorium','Fort San','sk','inst','standing',50.7946,-103.8099,'site','view','drive','','1912–1971',
  'Saskatchewan\'s first tuberculosis sanatorium, built from 1912 to 1922 in a coulee on the north shore of Echo Lake near Fort Qu\'Appelle. It opened in October 1917, and at its busiest held 358 patients, more than half of them returned soldiers after the First World War. It is the only one of the province\'s three sanatoria still standing. It later housed a summer school of the arts and a conference centre. Sources give 1971 as the closing year as a sanatorium; the National Trust for Canada gives 1912 as the start of the work.',
  'Tudor Revival and Arts and Crafts buildings: the Main Lodge (1912–17), Dr. Jenner\'s residence (1916), the Administration Building (1919), the Nurse\'s Residence (1921–22) and the broad lawn the register calls "the bowl". Many other buildings were pulled down in 2012; only these four are protected. The National Trust listed the place as endangered; its newest note, from 2015, found it vacant and at risk of loss by neglect. What stands now was not checked.',
  'A hillside TB sanatorium of half-timbered lodges above Echo Lake.',
  'Private land; the National Trust named the owner as a development company in 2015, and the present owner was not checked. Look from the public roads of the Resort Village of Fort San; the Trans Canada Trail passes through the village (village website). Do not enter the buildings.',
  [HP('Echo Valley Conference Centre (Fort San)',13889),L('National Trust for Canada: Fort Qu\'Appelle Sanatorium','https://nationaltrustcanada.ca/nt-endangered-places/fort-quappelle-sanatorium'),W('Fort San')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('larson-mausoleum-lang','Larson Mausoleum','Lang','sk','burial','standing',49.9169,-104.362,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1923–1925',
  'A stone tomb built in the early 1920s in the Lang cemetery for the land dealer Bernard Larson and his family. It is one of the few stone mausoleums in Saskatchewan and, unlike the others, was designed by the Regina architects Storey and Van Egmond. Larson helped bring the Soo Line railway to this ground at the start of the century.',
  'A plain rectangular building of Tyndall Stone, far grander than the other graves in the cemetery. It stands in a prominent place in the village cemetery. Not open inside.',
  'A limestone tomb on the prairie, built by a man who steered a railway to his land.',
  'Lang municipal cemetery (the register places it in the municipal cemetery; the map shows no public-land layer for it), open ground; go by day and keep to the paths. Parking on the road by the cemetery (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Larson Mausoleum',1313)],conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('weyburn-soo-line-museum','Soo Line Historical Museum (Weyburn\'s 1910 power plant)','Weyburn','sk','industry','standing',49.6586,-103.851,'exact','open','drive','','1910–',
  'Weyburn built its own power station in 1909 to 1910, a one-storey brick building with a tall chimney, to supply the town that grew up after the Soo Line (the Canadian Pacific\'s line through the south) arrived. The plant began with a 75-kilowatt generator for under fifty customers and served over 2,500 when SaskPower bought it in 1960. It is now a railway and local history museum.',
  'The register describes a one-storey red-brick building with a brick smokestack; all of it is brick from Estevan, and its plain look is typical of early industrial buildings in the province. Beside it the museum keeps railway cars and local history; what is displayed was not checked.',
  'A red-brick power house with its chimney, turned into a museum of the Soo Line.',
  'Run by the City of Weyburn at 411 Riverfront Road on Highway 39 (the address in the register is "Highway 39 SE"). In 2026 it opened by appointment only; admission was free. Hours and the telephone number are from the city\'s page, read in November 2026.',
  [HP('Soo Line Historical Museum',1592),L('City of Weyburn: Soo Line Historical Museum','https://weyburn.ca/soo-line-historical-museum/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',th=None,thn=None,hours='2026 season: by appointment only, free; closed fall and winter. Call 306-848-3278 to book'),
N('melville-intercession-church','Intercession of St. Mary Ukrainian Greek Orthodox Church','Melville','sk','worship','standing',50.9281,-102.796,'exact','view','drive','','1928',
  'A wooden church built in 1928 by Ukrainian Orthodox settlers who wanted to keep their faith in their new country. It is a municipal heritage property.',
  'A wood-frame church on a cross plan in the traditional Ukrainian Orthodox style, with three metal domes: two on twin towers by the front door and a larger one over the crossing. Interior not checked.',
  'A three-domed wooden Ukrainian church.',
  'At 398 Prince Edward Street, Melville. An active parish was not confirmed and no service times were found; look from the street. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Intercession of St. Mary Ukrainian Greek Orthodox Church',1577)],conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('canora-holy-trinity-church','Holy Trinity Ukrainian Greek Orthodox Church','Canora','sk','worship','standing',51.6315,-102.438,'exact','view','drive','','1928',
  'A Ukrainian Greek Orthodox parish was formed at Canora in 1919 and built this church in 1928, designed by Temish Pavlychenko. The parish built a larger church in 1963, but the old one is still used from time to time.',
  'Byzantine in style, with three domes set on towers, modelled on the churches of Kiev. The register says the inside is richly decorated and that the iconostasis (the icon screen), put in in 1944, is richly decorated with Ukrainian Orthodox icons. Not open to visitors on any schedule found.',
  'A domed 1928 church with a 1944 icon screen inside.',
  'At 710 Main Street, Canora. No visiting times found; look from the street, and go in only by arrangement with the parish. Parking on the street (not checked on site).',
  [HP('Holy Trinity Ukrainian Greek Orthodox Church',5813)],conf='mixed',brick='?',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('muenster-hotel','Muenster Hotel','Muenster','sk','hotel','standing',52.1899,-104.997,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1905',
  'A two-storey wooden hotel built in 1905 beside the railway by Mike Schmidt to feed and lodge settlers and travellers, many of them connected with nearby St. Peter\'s Abbey, the first Benedictine abbey in Canada. It is one of the oldest buildings in Muenster and, says the register, still runs as a hotel, dining room and lounge.',
  'A large plain two-storey building, wood-framed according to the register and now painted or stuccoed. The inside was not checked.',
  'A 1905 railway hotel still serving meals.',
  'A working hotel at 401 Railway Street, Muenster. Opening times were not found; ring ahead.',
  [HP('Muenster Hotel',9248)],conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours='?'),
N('fort-pelly-ii-site','Fort Pelly (the second fort) National Historic Site','Pelly','sk','fort','traces',51.7767,-101.999,'site','public','drive','forts','1793–1912',
  'The Hudson\'s Bay Company founded Fort Pelly in 1793 and built this second fort on higher ground in 1856, under Chief Factor W. J. Christie. It was the headquarters of the company\'s Swan River District for almost half a century and had a strong farming side. It was given up in 1912, and in 1921 it was sold and every building torn down or carried away. It was named a National Historic Site in 1953.',
  'Nothing stands. The register says cellar depressions and other marks of the stockade and its buildings (the Chief Factor\'s house, the fur store, the warehouse and trader\'s store, a stable and an ice house) can be seen in the ground on a rise above the Assiniboine River. Parks Canada says the site is unattended and takes an hour or more to walk on a self-guided visit.',
  'A fur-trade headquarters whose cellars are all that is left.',
  'Parks Canada\'s site, about 5 km west and 10 km south of the town of Pelly, off Provincial Highway 8. Parks Canada\'s page shows a fee section; its terms were not read. The page gives a point at 51.84, -102.048; the register\'s point, used here, is 8 km away, and the pin may be on the wrong side of the river: it was checked on the aerial only. The earlier Fort Pelly (the first fort) has a separate provincial designation, not visited here.',
  [HP('Fort Pelly National Historic Site of Canada',11745),L('Parks Canada: Fort Pelly, plan your visit','https://parks.canada.ca/lhn-nhs/sk/pelly/visit'),W('Fort Pelly')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('cumberland-house-park','Cumberland House Provincial Historic Park (the powder magazine)','Cumberland House','sk','fort','standing',53.9595,-102.265,'area','public','drive','forts','1793–1965',
  'Cumberland House was the Hudson\'s Bay Company\'s first inland trading post, set up by Samuel Hearne in 1774 on Cumberland Lake and moved about 2 km west in 1793 to ground on and next to this park. It traded until 1965 and is the oldest settlement in Saskatchewan that has been lived in without a break. The post lost its place as the inland headquarters after the company joined the North West Company in 1821.',
  'A small square stone powder magazine with a pyramid roof, built around 1886 for the company\'s store of gunpowder: one of few left in the province. The park also shows boilers and other iron from the company\'s steamer Northcote, and the ground may hold remains of the post. Not checked on site.',
  'Saskatchewan\'s oldest settlement, with the stone powder house of its fur post.',
  'A 5.2-hectare provincial park in the northern village of Cumberland House, open ground. No hours or fees were found. The point is the register\'s for the park, so the pin marks the area and not the magazine; the first post of 1774 stood on the lake shore elsewhere.',
  [HP('Cumberland House Provincial Park',3111),L('Tourism Saskatchewan: Cumberland House Provincial Historic Park','https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/provincialpark/1421/cumberland-house-provincial-historic-park')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
N('grey-owl-cabin-ajawaan','Grey Owl\'s Cabin, Ajawaan Lake','Waskesiu Lake','sk','landmark','standing',54.1462,-106.4649,'area','public','hike','','1931–1932',
  'The log cabin on the shore of Ajawaan Lake in Prince Albert National Park where the writer and conservationist Grey Owl (Archibald Belaney) lived with his tame beavers from 1932 until his death in 1938. A second, upper cabin was built in 1932 for his wife Anahareo, their daughter Shirley Dawn and visitors. The three are buried in a small graveyard beside it. Sources give the cabin\'s date as 1931 or 1932.',
  'A simple one-room log cabin with a gable roof, a porch and a roof ladder, in the style of a log-cabin tradition. Parks Canada has repaired it several times, and part of the beaver lodge in the lower cabin is a reconstruction. The graves lie by the upper cabin overlooking the lake. Signs tell the story.',
  'A hike or paddle to the log cabin of the man who made beavers famous.',
  'Parks Canada. The route starts at the Kingsmere River day-use lot, 32.5 km from Waskesiu on Kingsmere Road, and is 20 km each way on foot, or a paddle across Kingsmere Lake and a short portage into Ajawaan Lake. Overnight visitors must register at the Waskesiu information centre and get a park permit with camping fees; day-use rules and park entry fees were not checked. The pin is the lake\'s mapped point on the north shore, not the cabin.',
  [HP('Grey Owl\'s Cabin',4496),L('Parks Canada: routes to Grey Owl\'s Cabin','https://www.parks.canada.ca/pn-np/sk/princealbert/activ/experiences/randonee-hiking/cabane-cabin'),W('Ajawaan Lake')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=None,thn=None,hours=''),
